1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,851
An open-source evaluation harness wants to
show benchmark scores on contributor pull

2
00:00:09,891 --> 00:00:15,177
requests. The scores need a provider API
key. For every outside contributor the job

3
00:00:15,217 --> 00:00:17,423
fails, because the key isn't there.

4
00:00:17,763 --> 00:00:22,705
A maintainer searches for the error, finds
an answer with many upvotes, and changes

5
00:00:22,745 --> 00:00:27,267
one word in the trigger, the line that
says which event starts the workflow:

6
00:00:27,307 --> 00:00:31,049
pull_request becomes pull_request_target.
The check goes green.

7
00:00:31,089 --> 00:00:35,910
The contributor is happy. The maintainer
has handed the API key, and a read-write

8
00:00:35,950 --> 00:00:39,572
token for the repository, to
anyone who opens a pull request.

9
00:00:39,912 --> 00:00:44,709
The failing check was the security model
working. The one-word change removed it.

10
00:00:44,749 --> 00:00:49,666
In this video you learn exactly what that
word changes, and which line, usually two

11
00:00:49,706 --> 00:00:54,324
steps further down, completes the
vulnerability. Keep those two steps in mind.

12
00:00:56,164 --> 00:00:59,911
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:59,951 --> 00:01:04,754
In the last video you saw the model in
five parts. This video is parts two and

14
00:01:04,794 --> 00:01:07,920
three: fork pull requests,
and privileged triggers.

15
00:01:08,260 --> 00:01:13,300
A reminder of four words. A workflow is a
file that says: when this event happens,

16
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:16,769
run these jobs, each on a
fresh machine called a runner.

17
00:01:16,809 --> 00:01:20,300
A fork is a second repository
on GitHub, made from yours.

18
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:23,521
A pull request proposes
merging a branch into yours.

19
00:01:23,561 --> 00:01:27,176
A secret is an encrypted value
stored in GitHub's settings.

20
00:01:27,516 --> 00:01:32,104
Part two is a protection. A workflow triggered
by pull_request from a fork runs the

21
00:01:32,144 --> 00:01:36,676
contributor's version of the code with a
read-only token and without your secrets.

22
00:01:36,716 --> 00:01:41,193
You'll learn what that protects and what
it leaves exposed, and what the approval

23
00:01:41,233 --> 00:01:44,148
gate for first-time contributors
does and doesn't do.

24
00:01:44,488 --> 00:01:48,223
Part three is where the protection
is switched off on purpose.

25
00:01:48,263 --> 00:01:53,276
pull_request_target runs your workflow with
your token and your secrets in response

26
00:01:53,316 --> 00:01:56,685
to a stranger's pull request.
That can be exactly right.

27
00:01:56,725 --> 00:01:59,851
It becomes a vulnerability
at one identifiable step.

28
00:02:00,191 --> 00:02:05,211
This video is defensive, like the chapter.
You'll see the unsafe pattern as GitHub's

29
00:02:05,251 --> 00:02:09,067
own documentation prints it, marked
insecure, next to its fixes.

30
00:02:09,107 --> 00:02:14,007
You won't see anything that could be used
as it stands, and nothing is run against

31
00:02:14,047 --> 00:02:14,428
GitHub.

32
00:02:16,268 --> 00:02:17,847
Here's what you'll walk away with.

33
00:02:18,187 --> 00:02:19,599
After this video you can:

34
00:02:19,939 --> 00:02:24,903
explain why a fork pull request under
pull_request gets no secrets and a read-only

35
00:02:24,943 --> 00:02:26,856
token, and what remains exposed;

36
00:02:27,196 --> 00:02:30,525
say what the approval gate for
first-time contributors does;

37
00:02:30,865 --> 00:02:35,917
explain what pull_request_target and
workflow_run change: whose workflow file, which

38
00:02:35,957 --> 00:02:37,190
token, which secrets;

39
00:02:37,530 --> 00:02:41,072
name the step that turns a privileged
trigger into a vulnerability;

40
00:02:41,412 --> 00:02:46,903
state when pull_request_target is the right
trigger and what changes on 2 November

41
00:02:46,943 --> 00:02:48,926
2026, as the section gives it.

42
00:02:50,766 --> 00:02:55,445
Fork pull requests. In one sentence: a
workflow triggered by pull_request from a

43
00:02:55,485 --> 00:03:00,282
fork runs the contributor's version of the
code with a read-only token and without

44
00:03:00,322 --> 00:03:01,048
your secrets.

45
00:03:01,388 --> 00:03:06,322
Precisely. The documentation: "With the
exception of GITHUB_TOKEN, secrets are not

46
00:03:06,362 --> 00:03:10,870
passed to the runner when a workflow
is triggered from a forked repository.

47
00:03:10,910 --> 00:03:15,176
The GITHUB_TOKEN has read-only
permissions in pull requests from forked

48
00:03:15,216 --> 00:03:19,846
repositories." Pull requests opened by
Dependabot, GitHub's dependency update

49
00:03:19,886 --> 00:03:21,908
feature, are treated the same way.

50
00:03:22,248 --> 00:03:26,872
The approval gate. Whether such a run
starts at all is a separate setting.

51
00:03:26,912 --> 00:03:32,167
For public repositories, by default, all
first-time contributors require approval to

52
00:03:32,207 --> 00:03:37,335
run workflows. The options are approval
for first-time contributors who are new to

53
00:03:37,375 --> 00:03:41,999
GitHub, for all first-time contributors,
or for all external contributors.

54
00:03:42,339 --> 00:03:47,208
The documentation attaches a warning. A
user who has had any commit or pull request

55
00:03:47,248 --> 00:03:50,342
merged into the repository
will not require approval.

56
00:03:50,382 --> 00:03:54,954
And a malicious user could meet that
requirement by getting a small typing fix

57
00:03:54,994 --> 00:03:59,685
accepted. So the gate at its default is
a first-contact check, not a per-request

58
00:03:59,725 --> 00:04:00,099
review.

59
00:04:00,439 --> 00:04:04,773
What the fork model protects. Your
secrets: they aren't sent to the runner.

60
00:04:04,813 --> 00:04:07,048
Your repository: the token can't write.

61
00:04:07,388 --> 00:04:12,376
What it doesn't protect. The runner itself:
the contributor's code still executes on

62
00:04:12,416 --> 00:04:17,404
it. Readable data: a public repository's
contents, and the caches a pull request may

63
00:04:17,444 --> 00:04:21,475
restore. A cache is a stored directory
that a later run can restore.

64
00:04:21,515 --> 00:04:26,383
A self-hosted runner, which is your machine.
And workflows on privileged triggers,

65
00:04:26,423 --> 00:04:30,992
which, in the documentation's words,
"will always run, regardless of approval

66
00:04:31,032 --> 00:04:31,591
settings".

67
00:04:31,931 --> 00:04:36,153
The textbook calls that last row the
hinge. The approval gate applies to

68
00:04:36,193 --> 00:04:39,349
pull_request. It doesn't
apply to pull_request_target.

69
00:04:39,689 --> 00:04:44,487
Quick quiz. A stranger's fork pull
request starts a pull_request run.

70
00:04:44,527 --> 00:04:48,904
What does the job hold? A, a
read-write token and your secrets.

71
00:04:48,944 --> 00:04:52,339
B, a read-only token and
no secrets. Your answer?

72
00:04:57,179 --> 00:05:00,129
B. The token can read, and
your secrets stay behind.

73
00:05:00,169 --> 00:05:02,947
But the stranger's code
still runs on the runner.

74
00:05:03,287 --> 00:05:08,192
Privileged triggers. In one sentence:
pull_request_target runs your workflow, from

75
00:05:08,232 --> 00:05:13,137
your default branch, with your token and
your secrets, in response to a stranger's

76
00:05:13,177 --> 00:05:18,082
pull request. It's safe exactly as long as
it never executes that stranger's code.

77
00:05:18,122 --> 00:05:21,761
The default branch is the branch
a repository presents first.

78
00:05:22,101 --> 00:05:27,238
Precisely. GitHub's reference says such
workflows "run with elevated trust: the job

79
00:05:27,278 --> 00:05:31,729
receives the base repository's
GITHUB_TOKEN and access to repository and

80
00:05:31,769 --> 00:05:36,470
organization secrets". The base repository
is the one that receives the pull

81
00:05:36,510 --> 00:05:41,709
request. The trigger is safe by default
because the workflow, and any later checkout

82
00:05:41,749 --> 00:05:46,637
that doesn't specify a ref, is taken from
the base repository's default branch.

83
00:05:46,977 --> 00:05:50,965
A checkout fetches code onto the
runner, and a ref says which commit.

84
00:05:51,005 --> 00:05:55,811
Then the sentence that locates the risk:
"You introduce risk when a workflow author

85
00:05:55,851 --> 00:05:58,555
overrides this default
to run the fork's code."

86
00:05:58,895 --> 00:06:03,306
And the sentence that locates the step.
Quoting the reference: "The checkout step

87
00:06:03,346 --> 00:06:07,756
alone does not execute untrusted code… The
vulnerability is completed by the next

88
00:06:07,796 --> 00:06:11,493
step that runs code checked out into
the current working directory."

89
00:06:11,533 --> 00:06:16,054
The documentation gives this pattern a name
and says it "has been the root cause of

90
00:06:16,094 --> 00:06:17,977
multiple supply-chain compromises".

91
00:06:18,317 --> 00:06:22,330
The token isn't read-only here. The
workflow syntax reference: under

92
00:06:22,370 --> 00:06:27,097
pull_request_target the token "is granted
read/write repository permission, even

93
00:06:27,137 --> 00:06:29,540
when it is triggered from a public fork".

94
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:33,190
The unsafe pattern, as the
documentation prints it.

95
00:06:33,230 --> 00:06:38,444
The checkout line with @v6 is the
documentation's own example, quoted unchanged.

96
00:06:38,484 --> 00:06:43,896
The workflows of this course pin the checkout
action at version 7.0.1 by commit ID,

97
00:06:43,936 --> 00:06:49,150
and since version 7 and its back-ports
the action refuses this checkout unless a

98
00:06:49,190 --> 00:06:50,595
specific input is set.

99
00:06:50,935 --> 00:06:54,626
Read it with the question from
the last video. Whose code runs?

100
00:06:54,666 --> 00:06:58,772
make test runs the Makefile of the
pull request, so the contributor's.

101
00:06:58,812 --> 00:07:03,570
What can the job reach? A read-write token,
and every repository and organization

102
00:07:03,610 --> 00:07:08,427
secret the workflow names. What an attacker
achieves is whatever those credentials

103
00:07:08,467 --> 00:07:13,402
allow. There's the line from the opening:
not the trigger, not the checkout, but the

104
00:07:13,442 --> 00:07:14,231
step after it.

105
00:07:14,571 --> 00:07:18,514
And the textbook widens one word.
"Run" is wider than it looks.

106
00:07:18,554 --> 00:07:22,498
A build. An install step with
lifecycle scripts. A test runner.

107
00:07:22,538 --> 00:07:26,797
A linter that loads a configuration
file from the working directory.

108
00:07:26,837 --> 00:07:30,275
Each executes or is steered
by files from the checkout.

109
00:07:30,615 --> 00:07:34,080
The shapes to look for in
review, from the same reference.

110
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:39,036
Checking out the pull request's head or
merge commit, through a ref built from the

111
00:07:39,076 --> 00:07:43,328
event. Pointing the checkout at the
fork, through the repository input.

112
00:07:43,368 --> 00:07:48,163
Or fetching the code another way and
running it: a git fetch of the pull request

113
00:07:48,203 --> 00:07:51,910
ref, gh pr checkout, or downloading
an artifact built from it.

114
00:07:51,950 --> 00:07:55,718
An artifact is a set of files a
job uploads for later download.

115
00:07:56,058 --> 00:08:01,127
And the scope is wider than one trigger.
The reference: such patterns "are also not

116
00:08:01,167 --> 00:08:05,868
unique to pull_request_target… an
issue_comment or workflow_run workflow that

117
00:08:05,908 --> 00:08:10,485
fetches and runs a fork's pull request
code is vulnerable in the same way."

118
00:08:10,525 --> 00:08:15,656
For workflow_run, remember from video 143:
it's about the default branch, and it can

119
00:08:15,696 --> 00:08:18,795
read secrets even when the
first workflow couldn't.

120
00:08:18,835 --> 00:08:23,351
Its privilege comes from its position,
not from the run that triggered it.

121
00:08:23,691 --> 00:08:25,376
The fix, in order of preference.

122
00:08:25,716 --> 00:08:30,370
One. Don't use the trigger. The
reference: "If the workflow does not need

123
00:08:30,410 --> 00:08:35,385
pull_request_target, update it to use a
safer event where appropriate, such as

124
00:08:35,425 --> 00:08:40,657
pull_request." Most CI, the automated
testing of each change, needs no secrets and

125
00:08:40,697 --> 00:08:41,621
no write token.

126
00:08:41,961 --> 00:08:46,253
Two. If a privileged action is needed,
such as a comment or a label, keep

127
00:08:46,293 --> 00:08:50,466
pull_request_target but never check
out or run the pull request's code.

128
00:08:50,506 --> 00:08:55,154
Operate on metadata only, facts about the
pull request, not its files, with the

129
00:08:55,194 --> 00:08:59,961
narrowest permissions. This is the case
in which pull_request_target is the right

130
00:09:00,001 --> 00:09:00,435
trigger.

131
00:09:00,775 --> 00:09:05,818
Three. If the privileged action needs a
result computed from the contributor's code,

132
00:09:05,858 --> 00:09:10,538
separate the two. GitHub Security Lab's
design is an unprivileged pull_request

133
00:09:10,578 --> 00:09:15,076
workflow that builds and tests and
uploads its result as an artifact, and a

134
00:09:15,116 --> 00:09:19,553
privileged workflow_run workflow that
downloads the artifact and comments.

135
00:09:19,593 --> 00:09:24,273
Artifact data is safe there when it's
used, in the article's words, "in a safe

136
00:09:24,313 --> 00:09:29,174
manner, like reading PR numbers or reading
a code coverage text to comment on the

137
00:09:29,214 --> 00:09:29,416
PR".

138
00:09:29,756 --> 00:09:34,880
A label as a gate, "run only when a maintainer
adds a certain label", is weaker than

139
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:39,860
it looks. The Security Lab article notes
it's still prone to a race condition, in

140
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:45,024
which the attacker may push new changes
after the workflow was approved by the label

141
00:09:45,064 --> 00:09:46,438
but hasn't started yet.

142
00:09:46,778 --> 00:09:51,325
The platform now leans against the pattern.
Four changes matter in review, and the

143
00:09:51,365 --> 00:09:54,905
textbook is exact that none of
them removes the need for review.

144
00:09:55,245 --> 00:10:00,443
The eighth of December 2025: the workflow
file for pull_request_target always comes

145
00:10:00,483 --> 00:10:05,743
from the default branch. What it doesn't
cover: a vulnerable workflow that is on the

146
00:10:05,783 --> 00:10:06,690
default branch.

147
00:10:07,030 --> 00:10:12,715
actions/checkout version 7, the eighteenth
of June 2026, back-ported to older lines

148
00:10:12,755 --> 00:10:18,233
on the twentieth of July 2026: the action
refuses to fetch a fork pull request's

149
00:10:18,273 --> 00:10:23,752
head or merge ref under pull_request_target,
and under workflow_run started by a

150
00:10:23,792 --> 00:10:28,235
pull request event, unless
allow-unsafe-pr-checkout: true is set.

151
00:10:28,275 --> 00:10:34,029
What it doesn't cover: a git fetch or gh pr
checkout in a run step, and other events

152
00:10:34,069 --> 00:10:35,547
such as issue_comment.

153
00:10:35,887 --> 00:10:40,352
Read-only caches for low-trust triggers,
the twenty-sixth of June 2026: a

154
00:10:40,392 --> 00:10:44,178
privileged-trigger run can't
write the default branch's cache.

155
00:10:44,218 --> 00:10:48,313
What it doesn't cover: restoring a
cache that was already poisoned.

156
00:10:48,653 --> 00:10:53,478
And workflow execution protections,
generally available on the seventeenth of

157
00:10:53,518 --> 00:10:58,722
September 2026. GitHub announced that a
default rule will block pull_request_target

158
00:10:58,762 --> 00:11:03,903
in public repositories from the second of
November 2026 unless a maintainer allows

159
00:11:03,943 --> 00:11:09,021
it. Check the changelog on the day you
watch this. What it doesn't cover: private

160
00:11:09,061 --> 00:11:10,663
and internal repositories.

161
00:11:11,003 --> 00:11:15,798
One input deserves a sentence of its own.
The changelog says the opt-out flag "is

162
00:11:15,838 --> 00:11:20,275
intentionally named to be easy to spot
in code review and static analysis".

163
00:11:20,315 --> 00:11:25,050
The reference says: "Only do this after
confirming the checked-out code is never

164
00:11:25,090 --> 00:11:29,647
executed." In a pull request, the line
allow-unsafe-pr-checkout: true needs a

165
00:11:29,687 --> 00:11:30,960
written justification.

166
00:11:32,800 --> 00:11:37,387
Analogy for the fork model. A visitor
may use the lobby computer, which has no

167
00:11:37,427 --> 00:11:39,227
access to the internal network.

168
00:11:39,567 --> 00:11:44,347
It breaks in two places. By default a
visitor who has been in the building once is

169
00:11:44,387 --> 00:11:49,050
waved through without a check from then
on. And the lobby computer may be one of

170
00:11:49,090 --> 00:11:51,401
your own machines: a self-hosted runner.

171
00:11:51,741 --> 00:11:56,549
Analogy for the privileged trigger. The
front desk has a procedure for parcels from

172
00:11:56,589 --> 00:12:00,580
unknown senders: log the parcel, put
a sticker on it, send a receipt.

173
00:12:00,620 --> 00:12:04,201
The procedure is safe because
the desk never opens the parcel.

174
00:12:04,241 --> 00:12:08,932
The vulnerability is the day someone amends
the procedure to "open the parcel and

175
00:12:08,972 --> 00:12:13,372
follow the instructions inside", while
the clerk still holds the master key.

176
00:12:13,712 --> 00:12:18,505
Where that breaks: the parcel can also
influence the clerk without being opened.

177
00:12:18,545 --> 00:12:21,345
Its label is event text.
That's the next video.

178
00:12:21,685 --> 00:12:25,578
So carry two questions into every
review of a privileged trigger.

179
00:12:25,618 --> 00:12:29,028
Does anything bring the
stranger's files onto the runner?

180
00:12:29,068 --> 00:12:33,930
And does any later step execute or get
steered by files in the working directory?

181
00:12:33,970 --> 00:12:38,711
If both answers are yes, the trigger is
no longer safe, whatever the intentions

182
00:12:38,751 --> 00:12:39,013
were.

183
00:12:40,853 --> 00:12:45,335
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper.
Draw two columns, pull_request and

184
00:12:45,375 --> 00:12:50,609
pull_request_target, and four rows: where
the workflow file is read from, the token,

185
00:12:50,649 --> 00:12:55,633
the secrets, the approval gate. Fill in
both columns, and say each row out loud.

186
00:13:05,173 --> 00:13:09,732
Now check your rows. The left lane
gives the stranger's code no privilege.

187
00:13:09,772 --> 00:13:13,896
The right lane gives privilege and,
by default, no stranger's code.

188
00:13:13,936 --> 00:13:19,117
The vulnerability is a workflow that takes
the token and secrets from the right lane

189
00:13:19,157 --> 00:13:20,795
and the code from the left.

190
00:13:22,635 --> 00:13:25,896
Step 1: the triggers, read
with this video's question.

191
00:13:26,236 --> 00:13:31,395
Two of the five files run with the base
repository's token in response to something

192
00:13:31,435 --> 00:13:36,594
an outsider can do: file 1, on an opened
issue, and file 2, on pull_request_target.

193
00:13:36,634 --> 00:13:41,605
Those two are where you now read every
line. File 4 uses plain pull_request: the

194
00:13:41,645 --> 00:13:43,172
left lane of the diagram.

195
00:13:46,512 --> 00:13:49,148
Step 2: every expression in the files.

196
00:13:49,488 --> 00:13:54,229
The lab calls this the wide scan: every
expression, with its line number, to be

197
00:13:54,269 --> 00:13:59,252
classified by hand. An expression is the
dollar-brace formula that Actions replaces

198
00:13:59,292 --> 00:14:04,275
with its value. In file 2, which line would
tell you whether the stranger's code is

199
00:14:04,315 --> 00:14:08,270
brought onto the runner? You know
the shape from the review table.

200
00:14:08,310 --> 00:14:09,238
Say it out loud.

201
00:14:14,078 --> 00:14:18,713
Line 28 of file 2: a ref built from
the head commit of the pull request.

202
00:14:18,753 --> 00:14:24,167
That's the first shape in the documentation's
table: checking out the pull request's

203
00:14:24,207 --> 00:14:26,244
head. Under pull_request_target.

204
00:14:26,584 --> 00:14:31,322
Remember what the reference says about
that line: the checkout step alone doesn't

205
00:14:31,362 --> 00:14:34,447
execute untrusted code. So
the review isn't finished.

206
00:14:34,487 --> 00:14:39,048
You open the file and read what comes
after the checkout, and ask of each step

207
00:14:39,088 --> 00:14:42,822
whether it runs or is steered by
files in the working directory.

208
00:14:42,862 --> 00:14:47,482
That reading is yours to do in the lab.
And you look for one more line near the

209
00:14:47,522 --> 00:14:51,315
checkout, the one that the changelog
says is named to be spotted.

210
00:14:51,655 --> 00:14:54,267
Step 3: a scan that looks convincing.

211
00:14:54,607 --> 00:14:59,773
Exit status 1: no match. A reviewer who
stops here reports that no script contains

212
00:14:59,813 --> 00:15:05,107
an expression. Why can that be wrong? Think
about how a multi-line script is written

213
00:15:05,147 --> 00:15:08,917
in YAML, the format of workflow
files. Make your prediction.

214
00:15:13,757 --> 00:15:16,717
Step 4: the lines of
every multi-line script.

215
00:15:17,057 --> 00:15:21,854
The awk program, a small text-processing
tool, prints expression lines that lie

216
00:15:21,894 --> 00:15:25,283
inside a block that starts
with run: and a vertical bar.

217
00:15:25,323 --> 00:15:30,242
One line comes back, in file 1. The narrow
scan missed it, because the expression

218
00:15:30,282 --> 00:15:33,732
isn't on the line that says
run:. It's on the line after.

219
00:15:33,772 --> 00:15:38,692
This is the subject of the next video. For
now the lesson is about method: a scan

220
00:15:38,732 --> 00:15:43,652
tells you where to look, and a scan that
returns nothing tells you only what that

221
00:15:43,692 --> 00:15:44,448
scan can see.

222
00:15:46,288 --> 00:15:47,936
Five mistakes to watch for.

223
00:15:48,276 --> 00:15:52,733
Switching to pull_request_target so that
fork pull requests get the secrets.

224
00:15:52,773 --> 00:15:57,527
Root cause: that trigger gives the job the
base repository's read-write token and

225
00:15:57,567 --> 00:16:02,380
secrets and is not subject to the approval
gate; the textbook lists this advice as

226
00:16:02,420 --> 00:16:05,694
outdated and as the vulnerability
class of this chapter.

227
00:16:06,034 --> 00:16:10,647
Treating the checkout of the pull request's
head as the whole problem, or as no

228
00:16:10,687 --> 00:16:15,240
problem. Root cause: the checkout alone
executes nothing; the vulnerability is

229
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:19,303
completed by the next step that runs
code from the working directory.

230
00:16:19,643 --> 00:16:24,556
Thinking only of make test as "running
code". Root cause: a build, an install with

231
00:16:24,596 --> 00:16:29,630
lifecycle scripts, a test runner or a linter
that loads a configuration file are all

232
00:16:29,670 --> 00:16:31,744
steered by files from the checkout.

233
00:16:32,084 --> 00:16:36,409
Relying on the approval gate. Root
cause: by default it covers first-time

234
00:16:36,449 --> 00:16:40,655
contributors only, and it does not
apply to privileged triggers at all.

235
00:16:40,995 --> 00:16:45,781
Using a label as the gate for a privileged
run. Root cause: the contributor can push

236
00:16:45,821 --> 00:16:49,745
new changes after the label was added
and before the workflow starts.

237
00:16:51,585 --> 00:16:56,494
Now, out of the lab. The textbook's case
was the hook: an open-source evaluation

238
00:16:56,534 --> 00:17:01,566
harness wants benchmark scores on contributor
pull requests, and the scores need a

239
00:17:01,606 --> 00:17:06,639
provider API key. Under pull_request the
key is absent, so the job fails for every

240
00:17:06,679 --> 00:17:11,341
outside contributor. The textbook's
comment: this is the fork model working.

241
00:17:11,681 --> 00:17:16,704
The wrong fix is the one-word change. The
right ones, as the textbook lists them:

242
00:17:16,744 --> 00:17:21,704
run the keyed evaluation after merge. Or
run it on demand, by a maintainer, on a

243
00:17:21,744 --> 00:17:23,517
branch inside the repository.

244
00:17:23,857 --> 00:17:28,378
Or split the work into an unprivileged
run and a privileged follow-up that never

245
00:17:28,418 --> 00:17:32,882
executes the contributor's code, and that
treats what the first run produced as

246
00:17:32,922 --> 00:17:36,702
data. It never executes it and never
interpolates it into a script.

247
00:17:37,042 --> 00:17:41,626
Each of the three costs something: later
feedback, a manual step, or a second

248
00:17:41,666 --> 00:17:46,610
workflow to maintain. That cost is the price
of not putting an outsider's code next

249
00:17:46,650 --> 00:17:47,751
to your credential.

250
00:17:49,591 --> 00:17:55,502
Your turn. Do Lab 29.1, "Find and
fix five planted weaknesses", in

251
00:17:55,542 --> 00:18:02,805
lab-manual/m29-actions-security.md. Part
A runs in labs/shell and needs no GitHub

252
00:18:02,845 --> 00:18:03,436
access.

253
00:18:03,776 --> 00:18:07,538
Collect the evidence with the scans,
then read each file completely.

254
00:18:07,578 --> 00:18:12,011
Before you change anything, write one row
per file: the line, the class, what an

255
00:18:12,051 --> 00:18:15,981
attacker could achieve and under which
condition, and the smallest fix.

256
00:18:16,021 --> 00:18:20,118
For the file with the privileged trigger,
predict which step completes the

257
00:18:20,158 --> 00:18:22,858
vulnerability before you
read below the checkout.

258
00:18:23,198 --> 00:18:28,595
The challenge is Exercise 29.2,
"The comment workflow", in

259
00:18:28,635 --> 00:18:32,158
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

260
00:18:33,998 --> 00:18:37,213
Read the question on screen.
Say your answer out loud.

261
00:18:42,053 --> 00:18:46,710
A strong answer describes the pattern in
terms of the two lanes: which trigger, what

262
00:18:46,750 --> 00:18:51,350
that trigger gives the job, what the workflow
then brings onto the runner, and what

263
00:18:51,390 --> 00:18:55,990
happens next. It quotes the idea that the
checkout alone executes nothing and names

264
00:18:56,030 --> 00:18:57,500
the kind of step that does.

265
00:18:57,840 --> 00:19:01,860
It says what an attacker can reach and
why the approval gate doesn't help.

266
00:19:01,900 --> 00:19:06,469
The follow-up notes that the checkout action
has refused that checkout since version

267
00:19:06,509 --> 00:19:10,968
7 and asks whether the pattern is closed.
Answer from the "what it does not cover"

268
00:19:11,008 --> 00:19:13,712
column, and say what you
still look for in review.

269
00:19:15,552 --> 00:19:16,479
Let's land this.

270
00:19:16,819 --> 00:19:18,479
You should now be able to say:

271
00:19:18,819 --> 00:19:23,862
Under pull_request, a fork's code runs
with a read-only token and no secrets; the

272
00:19:23,902 --> 00:19:27,565
runner, readable data and
restorable caches remain exposed.

273
00:19:27,905 --> 00:19:32,436
The approval gate covers first-time
contributors by default and does not apply to

274
00:19:32,476 --> 00:19:33,565
privileged triggers.

275
00:19:33,905 --> 00:19:38,778
pull_request_target takes the workflow from
the default branch and gives the job a

276
00:19:38,818 --> 00:19:41,534
read-write token and the
repository's secrets.

277
00:19:41,874 --> 00:19:46,641
The vulnerability is completed by the step
that runs code checked out from the pull

278
00:19:46,681 --> 00:19:50,753
request, and "run" includes builds,
installs, tests and tools that read

279
00:19:50,793 --> 00:19:53,128
configuration from the working directory.

280
00:19:53,468 --> 00:19:58,662
The trigger is right for metadata-only
work; GitHub announced that from 2 November

281
00:19:58,702 --> 00:20:03,960
2026 a default rule blocks it in public
repositories unless a maintainer allows it.

282
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:10,856
Read sections 21A.4 and
21A.5 of Chapter 21A.

283
00:20:11,196 --> 00:20:15,859
Today you learned to read a privileged
trigger in two lanes, and to point at the

284
00:20:15,899 --> 00:20:19,327
step that matters. Run the scans
yourself in the lab shell.

285
00:20:19,367 --> 00:20:22,795
Next time: script injection
through untrusted event fields.

286
00:20:22,835 --> 00:20:25,851
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

287
00:20:25,891 --> 00:20:27,262
See you in the next one.
